WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.880
High schools across the US are
boosting student access to college by

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.090
letting kids rack up college credit
while still in high school.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:16.590
The Indiana-based 21st Century Charter
School offers a distinctive model.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:22.199
While 95% of high schoolers who earn
college credit do so via AP or similar

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:27.210
high-school-based programs, 21C is
actually getting kids to campus. This

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:32.700
approach is challenging. 21C had to align
its schedule to local colleges, handle

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:38.370
transportation costs, and find funds for
textbooks and tuition. Plus, there's a

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.480
skepticism as to whether high schoolers
can do college work. But there are also

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.309
clear benefits: Getting kids on campus
helps bridge a crucial psychological

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:51.780
barrier, especially for first-generation
college goers. Students have earned more

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:57.210
than sixteen hundred college credits and
17 degrees, all at no cost to their

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:03.660
families. It's too early to suggest that
21C's model works, but it's a promising,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.140
practical strategy.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.740
What do you think of
letting high schoolers take classes on

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.880
college campuses? Let us know in the
comments. Also, let us know what other

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.420
topics you'd like our scholars to cover
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.540
subscribe for more research and videos
from AEI.

